Orange County is one of the best places to live in California. Its sandy beaches, lively neighborhoods, and diverse cuisine and culture make Orange County a sought-after place. But life can quickly go from fabulous to frustrating if you [end up with a defective vehicle](/blog/lemon-law-facts-and-stats-in-orange-county).

Thankfully, the California Lemon Law is on your side, even if you use leased vehicles in Orange County. That’s right, the Lemon law also applies to leased vehicles. You can file a claim if the leased vehicle is defective and seek appropriate compensation.

Let’s talk about why people use leased vehicles and how the Lemon law works for leased cars.

## Why Use Leased Vehicles in Orange County?

Many Californians use leased vehicles in Orange County. It’s a great way to get your favorite four-wheel drive, whether you are here for a few months or a lifetime. So, before we get into how the Lemon law works for leased cars, let’s understand why leasing makes sense.

- **Cost-Effective Option:** Leased vehicles in Orange County usually require lower monthly payments than regular car (loan) payments. Many leases need a low or no down payment. This makes leasing a more affordable option.

- **Drive the Latest Model:** With a lease, you can change your car/pickup every year. You can get a model that’s currently the hottest trend in Orange County. It’s cheaper, faster, and easier than buying a new car every few years.

- **Warranty and Maintenance:** Typically, leased vehicles are under the manufacturer’s warranty. That means you don’t have to shell out thousands on unexpected repairs and regular maintenance.

- **No Trade-In Hassles:** The dealership takes the vehicle back at the end of your lease. Some may offer to sell it at a runaway cost when your lease ends.

- **Tax Benefits:** Leasing a car or pickup in Orange County may offer tax benefits. You might be able to claim tax deductions if you use the leased vehicles for your business. It can considerably lower your net cost of leasing.

- **Avoid Ownership Hassles:** Another benefit of leased vehicles is no ownership hassle. The dealership takes care of everything from insurance to regular maintenance. Plus, the service and repair costs are covered under the warranty.

## The California Lemon Law for Leased Cars

The California lemon law also applies to leased cars. It protects consumers who lease vehicles, including cars, pickups, SUVs, vans, and trailers. The Lemon law for lease vehicles applies to the same extent as for the purchased vehicles. However, the leased vehicle should meet a few criteria to qualify for a claim under state law.

- The vehicle must have a substantial defect.

- The defect must’ve occurred within the original warranty period (the number of months or the mileage limit).

- The defect must significantly impair the vehicle’s use, value, or safety.

- The dealer or manufacturer must’ve made reasonable attempts to repair the vehicle and failed.

That’s how the Lemon law works for used vehicles in Orange County and the rest of the state. With the help of a trusted lemon law attorney, you can file a claim to seek compensation.

## Damages You Can Seek Under the California Lemon Law for Leased Cars

The [Lemon Law refund policy](/blog/the-components-of-a-lemon-law-refund-in-california) is virtually the same for leased vehicles, except for a few minor changes. For purchased lemon vehicles, one option is getting a replacement vehicle. That means you can get a vehicle of comparable value in exchange for the lemon. The other option is to seek a refund for the purchase prices minus a mileage offset.

However, some of these provisions in the California Lemon law are not for leased cars. That doesn’t mean you can seek compensation. When you lease a lemon in Orange County or the rest of California, you can claim the following damages:

- The down payment on your lease.

- Any monthly lease payments you’ve made to date.

- All remaining lease payments, according to the contract.

- Collateral charges like service contract fees, taxes, finance charges, and other relevant costs.

- Incidental costs, including towing, repairs, and rentals.

While you have the right to seek damages, you also have a few responsibilities as a lessee. For one, you must notify the dealership or manufacturer immediately and provide them with reasonable attempts to repair the defect. You should also [keep detailed records](/blog/what-records-are-needed-in-california-lemon-law-cases) of all your repairs, lease payments, and other expenses related to the defective vehicle.

